WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.038
Environmental risk assessment combines forecast information, a site survey, aircraft limits, crew capability, mission complexity, and current observations.

00:00:09.038 --> 00:00:17.613
It should produce explicit go or no-go criteria rather than a vague sense that conditions look acceptable.

00:00:17.613 --> 00:00:23.870
Check weather from appropriate current sources, then compare it with the site.

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:31.518
Observe wind indicators, gusts, visibility, precipitation, cloud, temperature, sun angle, terrain effects, and changing activity.

00:00:31.518 --> 00:00:34.994
Record material differences from the forecast.

00:00:34.994 --> 00:00:41.714
Survey launch, recovery, route, obstacles, people, vehicles, radio conditions, and emergency landing options.

00:00:41.714 --> 00:00:46.118
Identify hazards, consequence, likelihood, controls, and residual risk.

00:00:46.118 --> 00:00:50.057
Assign an owner for each important control.

00:00:50.057 --> 00:01:01.413
Brief the crew on the objective, limits, battery thresholds, communication terms, loss-of-link behavior, weather triggers, emergency areas, and who can call a stop.

00:01:01.413 --> 00:01:05.353
Shared expectations reduce delay when conditions change.

00:01:05.353 --> 00:01:07.902
Monitor trends during flight.

00:01:07.902 --> 00:01:14.622
Wind, visibility, battery performance, signal quality, lighting, and ground activity can deteriorate gradually.

00:01:14.622 --> 00:01:21.807
Use early thresholds that preserve time and energy for a controlled return and landing.

00:01:21.807 --> 00:01:24.819
Document the decision and outcome.

00:01:24.819 --> 00:01:30.149
A canceled flight can be evidence of good risk management.

00:01:30.149 --> 00:01:42.432
After the mission, compare expected and actual conditions, capture lessons, and adjust future margins without turning one successful flight into permission for less conservative behavior.
